The Line at One-Half — music video (Chalkboard style, Lemo-Opuscar)

MATHEMATICS (the subject of the film; nothing of it is reproduced here)
- The claimed proof of the Riemann Hypothesis and its Lean formalisation: Levent Alpöge, working with Claude,
  announced 2026-11-20. Author's outline: https://alpo.ge/remark.pdf · code: https://alpo.ge/ ·
  GitHub mirror: https://github.com/CocoSgt/riemann-hypothesis-lean. Status at the time of the film: no public
  independent build, kernel replay or review.
- Background facts on the board (Riemann 1859; Weil 1948; Stepanov 1969; the first zeros of ζ; the explicit formula;
  Weil's positivity criterion) are standard textbook material. The outline of the new argument (theta-lift chains of
  length about √X, Rallis seesaw and Bessel periods, Iwasawa towers, qN = M^k + R with iterated Parseval, the 2/3 stall,
  twisting, a weakened Vandiver, K-groups) is paraphrased from remark.pdf; the Lean figures (175,187 files, ≈17.4 M
  lines, 0 sorry, 0 axiom declarations) from the mirror's README.

MUSIC
- "The Line at One-Half", generated with Suno and supplied by the film's owner (audio/song.mp3). Used as the
  complete soundtrack, unedited.

IMAGES, FOOTAGE, MODELS
- Clawd is Anthropic's mascot for Claude Code; drawn here in chalk (js/clawd.js) as a nod to Claude's part in the
  proof. Its silhouette (10×6 body, four legs, two slit eyes) follows the mascot; the chalk rendering is this film's.
- Otherwise none. Wall, boards, frame, tray, clock and every chalk stroke are drawn in code. No photographs, no scans,
  no AI-generated images, no 3D models.

CHALK ENGINE
- js/chalk.js, js/hand.js, js/r2d.js, js/post.js, js/props.js, js/lab.js, js/hz.js, tools/foley_lib.py: from the
  Chalkboard style of the Lemo-Opuscar library (github.com/lemomo-ai/lemo-opuscar), used as the style's engine.
- js/mathhand.js (Greek letters and maths symbols as chalk centre lines), js/stage.js, js/app.js, js/film.js: written
  for this film. v2: js/fx.js, js/clawd.js, tools/beats.py written for this film; js/post.js extended (glow, flash,
  chromatic aberration, night grade).
- v2's motion grammar (beat punch-ins, decaying shakes, flash frames, escalating choruses) was studied from
  github.com/JohnHeibel/PDoomVideo; no code or art from it is used.

